{
  "teamId": 134,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Pittsburgh Pirates were a team in the earliest stages of a rebuild, and the season's 61-101 record made that painful reality impossible to ignore. Scoring just 609 runs against a pitching staff that surrendered 833, Pittsburgh was outmatched on most nights. Yet within that difficult year, individual moments of genuine excitement offered glimpses of what the organization hoped to build toward — particularly from catcher Jacob Stallings, outfielder Bryan Reynolds, and a collection of fringe contributors who flashed unexpected life.\n\nThe season began with modest promise in April, where the Pirates actually finished above .500 at 12-13, before May's 8-20 collapse set the tone for what was to come. That brutal May featured Richard Rodríguez imploding in key relief spots, surrendering crushing hits that erased late leads. But even in those dark moments, there were bright spots — Adam Frazier's clutch triple and Stallings's timely home run in mid-May showed the team could compete on individual nights.\n\nJune and July offered the season's most memorable moments. Jacob Stallings delivered the year's single biggest play on July 17th, launching a grand slam worth nearly a full win in probability. That homer served as the emotional peak of a Pirates season that otherwise ground its players and fans down steadily. The trade of Adam Frazier to San Diego in mid-July symbolized the organizational pivot: this was not a team trying to win in 2021, but one trying to identify pieces for the future.\n\nThe final two months were a dispiriting conclusion, with the bullpen — particularly Chasen Shreve, Chris Stratton, and Nick Mears — repeatedly surrendering leads in high-leverage situations. Still, rookies like Hoy Park and Ke'Bryan Hayes (when healthy) offered tantalizing flashes. The Pirates finished with a losing record in every month except April and October, a 101-loss season that, as much as it hurt, cleared the runway for a new chapter in Pittsburgh baseball.",
  "chapters": [
